Topics: numerical functions and sequences.

This exercise is a multiple-choice questionnaire. For each of the following questions, exactly one of the four proposed answers is correct. No justification is required.

For questions 1 to 3 below, consider a function $f$ defined and twice differentiable on $\mathbb{R}$. The curve of its derivative $f'$ is given in the figure.
We accept that $f'$ has a maximum at $-\dfrac{3}{2}$ and that its curve meets the $x$-axis at the point with coordinates $\left(-\dfrac{1}{2},0\right)$.
Recall that the curve in the figure represents the derivative $f'$ of $f$.

Question 1:
(a) The function $f$ has a maximum at $-\dfrac{3}{2}$;
(b) The function $f$ has a maximum at $-\dfrac{1}{2}$;
(c) The function $f$ has a minimum at $-\dfrac{1}{2}$;
(d) At the point with abscissa $-1$, the curve of the function $f$ has a horizontal tangent.

Question 2:
(a) The function $f$ is convex on $\left(-\infty,-\dfrac{3}{2}\right)$;
(b) The function $f$ is convex on $\left(-\infty,-\dfrac{1}{2}\right)$;
(c) The curve $\mathcal{C}_f$ representing the function $f$ has no inflection point;
(d) The function $f$ is concave on $\left(-\infty,-\dfrac{1}{2}\right)$.

Question 3:
The second derivative $f''$ of the function $f$ satisfies:
(a) $f''(x)\geqslant 0$ for $x\in\left(-\infty,-\dfrac{1}{2}\right)$;
(b) $f''(x)\geqslant 0$ for $x\in[-2,-1]$;
(c) $f''\left(-\dfrac{3}{2}\right)=0$;
(d) $f''(-3)=0$.

Question 4:
Consider three sequences $(u_n)$, $(v_n)$ and $(w_n)$. We know that, for every natural number $n$, $u_n\leqslant v_n\leqslant w_n$, and moreover $\displaystyle\lim_{n\to+\infty}u_n=1$ and $\displaystyle\lim_{n\to+\infty}w_n=3$.
We can then state that:
(a) the sequence $(v_n)$ converges;
(b) if the sequence $(u_n)$ is increasing, then the sequence $(v_n)$ is bounded below by $u_0$;
(c) $1\leqslant v_0\leqslant 3$;
(d) the sequence $(v_n)$ diverges.

Question 5:
Consider a sequence $(u_n)$ such that, for every nonzero natural number $n$, $u_n\leqslant u_{n+1}\leqslant\dfrac{1}{n}$.
We can then state that:
(a) the sequence $(u_n)$ diverges;
(b) the sequence $(u_n)$ converges;
(c) $\displaystyle\lim_{n\to+\infty}u_n=0$;
(d) $\displaystyle\lim_{n\to+\infty}u_n=1$.

Question 6:
Let $(u_n)$ be a real sequence such that for every natural number $n$, $n<u_n<n+1$.
We can state that:
(a) there exists a natural number $N$ such that $u_N$ is an integer;
(b) the sequence $(u_n)$ is increasing;
(c) the sequence $(u_n)$ is convergent;
(d) the sequence $(u_n)$ has no limit.

