This exercise is a multiple-choice questionnaire. For each of the following questions, exactly one of the four proposed answers is correct. No justification is required.

Space is equipped with an orthonormal coordinate system $\left(O;\overrightarrow{i},\overrightarrow{j},\overrightarrow{k}\right)$.
Consider:
the points $A(-1,-2,3)$, $B(1,-2,7)$ and $C(1,0,2)$;
the line $\Delta$ with parametric representation $\left\{\begin{array}{l}x=1-t\\ y=2\\ z=-4+3t\end{array}\right.$, where $t\in\mathbb{R}$;
the plane $\mathcal{P}$ with Cartesian equation $3x+2y+z-4=0$;
the plane $\mathcal{Q}$ with Cartesian equation $-6x-4y-2z+7=0$.

1. Which of the following points belongs to the plane $\mathcal{P}$?
(a) $R(1,-3,1)$
(b) $S(1,2,-1)$
(c) $T(1,0,1)$
(d) $U(2,-1,1)$

2. The triangle ABC is:
(a) equilateral;
(b) right-angled and isosceles;
(c) isosceles but not right-angled;
(d) right-angled but not isosceles.

3. The line $\Delta$ is:
(a) orthogonal to the plane $\mathcal{P}$;
(b) intersecting the plane $\mathcal{P}$;
(c) contained in the plane $\mathcal{P}$;
(d) strictly parallel to the plane $\mathcal{P}$.

4. We are given the dot product $\overrightarrow{BA}\cdot\overrightarrow{BC}=20$.
A measure, to the nearest degree, of the angle $\widehat{ABC}$ is:
(a) $34^{\circ}$
(b) $120^{\circ}$
(c) $90^{\circ}$
(d) $0^{\circ}$

5. The intersection of the planes $\mathcal{P}$ and $\mathcal{Q}$ is:
(a) a plane;
(b) the empty set;
(c) a line;
(d) reduced to a point.
